        </h3>
		%>
    </div>
	<br><p>I am a <strong>health economist and decision scientist</strong> specializing in evidence-to-decision research. I combine economic evaluation, causal inference, mathematical modeling, preference research, and real-world evidence to help organizations determine which interventions work, for whom, at what cost, and under what conditions.
	<br>
	<br>My work begins with a consequential policy or investment decision. I collaborate with stakeholders to identify evidence gaps, evaluate potential solutions, and characterize impact, affordability, equity, and uncertainty. I then translate the findings into actionable recommendations about how limited resources should be allocated and whether interventions should be adopted, adapted, or scaled.
	<br>
	<br><strong>Explore my work to see how rigorous analysis can turn evidence into better policies, investments, and outcomes.</strong>
	<!--
	<br> Use "<strong>... </strong>" to bold a phrase
	<br>The first aim of my dissertation, “Estimating HIV-prevalence point estimates and uncertainty bounds at the zip code level using existing data.” was selected for the Lee B. Lusted Student Competition in the 2021 Society for Medical Decision Making Conference.
	<br>
	<br>Prior to my doctoral training, I was a Researcher at the School of Public Health, University Cayetano Heredia, where I focused on cost-effective analyses and policy evaluation. I also served in the Peruvian Ministry of Health leading the creation of a healthcare services forecasting unit.
	-->
	<br>
	</blockquote>
